tcptarpit
=========

Post-installation steps.

1. The unprivileged daemon user _tcptarpit and its login class
   (/etc/login.conf.d/tcptarpit, openfiles 1024/4096) were created
   and registered automatically.

2. Enable and start the daemon:

       rcctl enable tcptarpit
       rcctl start tcptarpit

   It listens on 127.0.0.1 and ::1 (port 8024) and logs to
   /var/log/tcptarpit.log (created and rotated via newsyslog(8)
   automatically; /var/log/daemon stays clean).

3. pf(4) integration - without pf rules no traffic reaches the
   tarpit.  Minimal /etc/pf.conf (adjust ext_if and ports):

       ext_if = "vio0"
       table <bruteforcers> persist
       pass out on $ext_if proto { tcp, udp, icmp } all keep state
       pass in quick on $ext_if proto tcp from <bruteforcers> \
           to ($ext_if) port { http, https } rdr-to 127.0.0.1 port 8024
       pass in on $ext_if proto tcp to ($ext_if) port { http, https } \
           flags S/SA keep state \
           (max-src-conn-rate 30/10, overload <bruteforcers> flush)

   Then reload with: pfctl -f /etc/pf.conf

4. Optional: configuration in /etc/tcptarpit.conf (listen_port,
   max_age, max_per_source, stutter, ...).  IPv4 and IPv6 listeners
   are both on by default (127.0.0.1 and ::1) and individually
   switchable; IPv6 traffic needs the inet6 pf rules shown in
   tcptarpit(8).  At most five connections per source address are
   held (max_per_source), further ones are closed silently.

   Runtime statistics (who is trapped right now, counters, top
   sources, hold times):

       /etc/rc.d/tcptarpit statistic

5. Managing caught addresses:

       pfctl -t bruteforcers -T show
       pfctl -t bruteforcers -T delete <ip>

Further documentation: tcptarpit(8).
